WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:04.110
- I was the first person

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.130
to paddleboard the length of England,

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.250
and then I was the first female

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.350
to paddleboard across the English Channel.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.900
(light splashing)

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.510
Being on the water helps
me restore my health,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.840
and now I feel like I want
to give something back

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.260
and restore the health of the waterways

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.283
because they need it.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.723
Paddleboarding was my way to
tackle the plastic pollution.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.463
(upbeat music)

00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:44.963
(gentle lapping)

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.330
My name's Lizzie Carr.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.610
I'm an adventurer,
environmentalist and paddleboarder.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.910
I started paddleboarding

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.670
purely as a way of getting fit again

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.800
after I was diagnosed with cancer.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.800
The first time I put
my paddle in the water,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.340
it was like meditation.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.310
It was this really
calming, serene experience,

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.810
and then all of a
sudden, I'm on the water,

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.950
and I'm seeing plastic everywhere.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.530
On all the journeys I've done,

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.650
bottles are by far the most
common thing I've found.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.650
I think I've probably picked
up thousands of these.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.320
You see how big and how immediate

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.710
and close the problem is.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.970
Eighty percent of marine
debris starts inland,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.610
so effectively from our water ways,

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.570
before it flows out into the oceans.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.730
For me, it was always about

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.650
using adventure and paddleboarding

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.390
as a way to get people thinking

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.190
and talking about plastic pollution.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.000
I decided to paddleboard
the length of England

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.170
from its most southerly point

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.950
to its most northerly point

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.810
through the connected waterways network.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.840
I started in Godalming in Surrey,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.170
and I paddled 400 miles to
Kendal in Lake District.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.760
It took 22 days to complete,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.610
and I photographed and logged

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.050
every single piece of plastic

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.720
that I encountered on that route.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.080
I took over 3,000 photographs,

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.090
thousands and thousands of pieces.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.580
What I logged was a crazy amount,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.853
and that's not even a fraction of it.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.410
My next adventure was
paddleboarding from England

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.230
to France, so it was about 24 miles

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.740
across on the English Channel

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.010
and take water samples every fourth mile

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.740
to analyze micro-plastics.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:33.940
And if you look closely,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.210
you can find these tiny
pieces of micro-plastics

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:44.990
where it looks like styrofoam
has just broken down;

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.110
and obviously that never goes away,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.410
and that's when it gets really dangerous

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:51.510
because that's when marine
life confuses this for food

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.020
and they eat it.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.253
You can see it all over.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.150
This Saturday, we're
going out in North London,

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.335
and we're doing a big clean
up there with the community.

00:03:06.335 --> 00:03:08.600
Welcome to Plastic Patrol.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.250
And then we'll just get out on the water

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.360
and spend a couple of
hours just paddling around

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.670
and looking for litter and
putting it in the buckets

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.002
that we have at the front of the boards.

00:03:17.002 --> 00:03:19.370
Over the last few years of Plastic Patrol,

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.990
we've collected 189 ton
bags of plastic waste

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.320
and removed them from our water ways;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.220
and I look at every
single one like a victory.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.830
You're seeing the very best

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.630
and the very worst of humanity.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.140
The worst is represented
by all of the plastic

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.570
and the sheer volume of plastic

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.041
that you're there clearing up,

00:03:40.041 --> 00:03:42.983
and the best is the
people trying to fix that.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.770
I feel more proud of that

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.813
than I do of paddleboarding a distance.

